insert into public.posts (title, slug, category, publish_date, excerpt, content, featured_image, status, author) values
(
  'The Grammar of Peace: Language as a Tool for Conflict Resolution in West Africa',
  'the-grammar-of-peace',
  'Edupeace',
  '2025-01-15',
  'Language is not merely a vehicle for communication; it is a battleground where conflicts are waged and peace is negotiated. This essay examines how deliberate linguistic choices can de-escalate tensions across Nigeria''s ethno-linguistic diversity.',
  'Language is not merely a vehicle for communication; it is a battleground where conflicts are waged and peace is negotiated. This essay examines how deliberate linguistic choices can de-escalate tensions across Nigeria''s ethno-linguistic diversity.\n\nThe premise of Edupeace — education for peace — rests on a fundamental insight: that violence is, in part, a failure of imagination. When people cannot imagine how to resolve disagreement through words, they resort to force. The task of peace-oriented education is therefore to expand the repertoire of peaceful linguistic options available to communities in conflict.\n\nNigeria presents a compelling case study. With over 500 living languages and a colonial legacy that imposed English as the official medium of governance, the country''s linguistic landscape is a microcosm of global complexity. The Fulani-farmer clashes of the Middle Belt, the agitations in the Niger Delta, the Boko Haram insurgency in the Northeast — each conflict has a linguistic dimension that conventional security responses consistently overlook.\n\nMy research in pragmatics suggests that the choice of frame — the linguistic packaging of a dispute — significantly affects its trajectory. When herders and farmers meet in state-mediated dialogue, the language used by facilitators either reinforces tribal identities (and thus zero-sum thinking) or opens space for shared categories like "Nigerians," "farmers," or "rural communities." The difference is not semantic. It is life and death.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1776200816806-e3a579065d07?w=800&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format',
  'published',
  'Prof. Mahfouz A. Adedimeji'
),
(
  'Chinua Achebe at 100: A Retrospective on the Father of African Literature',
  'chinua-achebe-at-100',
  'Books Review',
  '2025-02-08',
  'A century after Achebe''s birth, his works remain not merely canonical but urgent. Things Fall Apart is less a novel than a civilizational argument — one that Africa is still winning.',
  'A century after Achebe''s birth, his works remain not merely canonical but urgent. Things Fall Apart is less a novel than a civilizational argument — one that Africa is still winning.\n\nWhen Achebe published Things Fall Apart in 1958, he was making a claim that the literary establishment of London was not fully prepared to hear: that Africans had complex, coherent, and morally serious civilizations before colonialism, and that the destruction of those civilizations was a tragedy, not a liberation.\n\nSixty-seven years later, that claim seems self-evident. Yet the work required to make it self-evident was enormous, and Achebe bore much of that burden himself. Through Arrow of God, No Longer at Ease, A Man of the People, and Anthills of the Savannah, he traced the entire arc of Igbo and Nigerian experience — from pre-colonial equilibrium through colonial disruption to post-independence disillusionment.\n\nWhat strikes the contemporary reader returning to these novels is their moral seriousness. Achebe does not simply invert the colonial gaze — he does not make Africans noble and Europeans villainous in the way that colonial literature made the reverse error. His characters are fully human: capable of greatness, pettiness, courage, and cowardice in equal measure.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1497633762265-9d179a990aa6?w=800&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format',
  'published',
  'Prof. Mahfouz A. Adedimeji'
),
(
  'Usmanu Danfodiyo University at Fifty: Between Promise and Performance',
  'usmanu-danfodiyo-university-at-fifty',
  'The Alma Mater',
  '2025-03-12',
  'To celebrate a university''s fiftieth anniversary is both a joy and an obligation — a joy because the institution has endured, and an obligation to be honest about where it stands relative to its founding ambitions.',
  'To celebrate a university''s fiftieth anniversary is both a joy and an obligation — a joy because the institution has endured, and an obligation to be honest about where it stands relative to its founding ambitions.\n\nUsmanu Danfodiyo University, Sokoto, was established in 1975 in the old Sokoto State, in the heart of the Sokoto Caliphate''s historic territory. It was named after the scholar-reformer whose 19th-century jihad transformed not just the political but the intellectual landscape of what is now Northern Nigeria. That name is a burden of sorts: an institution bearing the name of one of Africa''s great intellectuals carries an implicit mandate to honour that legacy through the quality of its intellectual production.\n\nI spent formative years within her walls. The university gave me not merely a degree but a sensibility — a way of approaching questions that has served me across disciplines and continents. For that, I am unreservedly grateful. But gratitude does not preclude honest assessment.\n\nThe challenges facing UDUS in 2025 are not unique to her — they are systemic afflictions that Nigerian public universities share. Chronic underfunding, brain drain, infrastructure decay, and the lingering effects of repeated ASUU strikes have taken a toll on academic quality across the board.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1576495199011-eb94736d05d6?w=800&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format',
  'published',
  'Prof. Mahfouz A. Adedimeji'
),
(
  'The African Renaissance Has Not Failed — It Has Been Deferred',
  'the-african-renaissance-has-not-failed',
  'Renaissance',
  '2025-04-03',
  'Thabo Mbeki''s dream of an African Renaissance was pronounced dead by its critics within a decade of its articulation. I argue it was never tried in the form Mbeki actually proposed.',
  'Thabo Mbeki''s dream of an African Renaissance was pronounced dead by its critics within a decade of its articulation. I argue it was never tried in the form Mbeki actually proposed.\n\nThe concept of an African Renaissance — the rebirth of African civilization, the reclamation of African dignity, the reconstruction of African institutions on African terms — predates Mbeki. It runs from Cheikh Anta Diop through Kwame Nkrumah to Leopold Senghor. But it was Mbeki who, in 1996, gave it the formal articulation that entered global policy discourse.\n\nMbeki''s vision was not simply economic development. It was civilizational. He spoke of Africans reclaiming their place as agents of history rather than its objects. He spoke of African institutions — governmental, scholarly, cultural — that could compete on the global stage without sacrificing their distinctiveness. He spoke, in short, of something genuinely radical: a continent that developed on its own terms.\n\nThe critics who pronounced this vision dead pointed to Zimbabwe''s collapse, to the failures of NEPAD, to the persistence of poverty and conflict across the continent. These criticisms are not wrong, but they mistake a specific political project for the entire idea.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1632215861513-130b66fe97f4?w=800&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format',
  'published',
  'Prof. Mahfouz A. Adedimeji'
),
(
  'On the Question of Language and Identity in a Multilingual Nation',
  'on-the-question-of-language-and-identity',
  'Give & Take',
  '2025-05-20',
  'A reader recently wrote to ask why I write primarily in English rather than Yoruba, given my advocacy for African languages. The question deserves a full answer.',
  'A reader recently wrote to ask why I write primarily in English rather than Yoruba, given my advocacy for African languages. The question deserves a full answer.\n\nThe short answer is: because this is where the readers are. The longer answer is more complicated, and it implicates structural inequalities that individual choice cannot resolve.\n\nNigerian publishing exists almost entirely in English. Academic journals of international standing conduct their business in English. The professoriate in which I operate communicates in English. To choose to write exclusively in Yoruba would be to accept a dramatically reduced audience — not as a matter of quality, but as a matter of infrastructure.\n\nThis is not a trivial trade-off. It is the central paradox facing African intellectuals who care about African languages: the languages that carry our deepest cultural heritage are, by the architecture of global knowledge production, systematically marginalized. To be heard beyond one''s immediate community, one must use the colonizer''s tongue.\n\nI do not accept this situation as permanent or natural. But I refuse the pretense that individual virtue — my writing in Yoruba — can substitute for the structural changes needed to make African languages viable media of intellectual exchange.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1567564586042-9173335de5f4?w=800&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format',
  'published',
  'Prof. Mahfouz A. Adedimeji'
),
(
  'Ramadan as Discipline: Reflections on the Pedagogy of Fasting',
  'ramadan-as-discipline',
  'Islam',
  '2025-03-30',
  'Fasting is among the oldest of human spiritual technologies. What Ramadan teaches — beyond hunger — is a discipline of attention that has applications far beyond its religious context.',
  'Fasting is among the oldest of human spiritual technologies. What Ramadan teaches — beyond hunger — is a discipline of attention that has applications far beyond its religious context.\n\nThe Arabic word for fasting, sawm, carries connotations beyond mere abstinence from food. It implies restraint, withholding, a deliberate pause. The fasting Muslim is not merely not eating; they are practicing a comprehensive self-governance that extends to speech, thought, and social conduct.\n\nFor thirty days each year, more than a billion people reorganize their lives around a rhythm that is fundamentally non-capitalist: waking before dawn to eat, breaking fast at sunset in community, spending the night hours in additional prayer and reflection. The market does not set this schedule. God does.\n\nI have found, over many years of Ramadan observance, that the month functions as an annual recalibration. The hunger is real and at times acute, but it is the mental clarity that surprises me each year — the sense, by the second or third week, of having cleared cognitive space that ordinary eating patterns somehow cluttered.\n\nContemporary neuroscience is beginning to understand what Muslim tradition has known for fourteen centuries: that intermittent fasting produces measurable cognitive benefits. But to reduce Ramadan to a health intervention is to miss the point entirely.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1590075865003-e48277faa558?w=800&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format',
  'published',
  'Prof. Mahfouz A. Adedimeji'
),
(
  'University Governance in Nigeria: Diagnosing the Crisis',
  'university-governance-in-nigeria',
  'Edupeace',
  '2024-11-05',
  'The crisis in Nigerian universities is not primarily financial. It is a crisis of governance — of accountability, vision, and the structures that make or break institutional integrity.',
  'The crisis in Nigerian universities is not primarily financial. It is a crisis of governance — of accountability, vision, and the structures that make or break institutional integrity.\n\nI speak from experience. Having served in senior university administration for several years, I have seen from the inside how governance failures compound and calcify into institutional dysfunction. The problems are not mysterious; they are well-documented. What is lacking is the political will — within and outside universities — to address them.\n\nThe first and most fundamental problem is the appointment process for vice-chancellors and other senior administrators. In too many Nigerian universities, these appointments are made not on the basis of academic distinction or administrative competence, but on the basis of political connections, ethnic affiliation, or financial inducement. An institution led by someone who attained their position through these means begins its tenure compromised.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495446815901-a7297e633e8d?w=800&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format',
  'published',
  'Prof. Mahfouz A. Adedimeji'
),
(
  'The Fulbright Experience: American Universities Through Nigerian Eyes',
  'the-fulbright-experience',
  'The Alma Mater',
  '2024-09-18',
  'A Fulbright scholarship is not merely an academic opportunity. It is an exercise in comparative civilization — an invitation to see one''s own country with fresh eyes by seeing another with the eyes of a careful stranger.',
  'A Fulbright scholarship is not merely an academic opportunity. It is an exercise in comparative civilization — an invitation to see one''s own country with fresh eyes by seeing another with the eyes of a careful stranger.\n\nI arrived in the United States with two decades of Nigerian academic life behind me and the kind of selective knowledge of American universities that reading and conference travel provide. What I found exceeded my expectations in some respects and fell below them in others — which is precisely the educational value of immersive experience over vicarious learning.\n\nAmerican universities are, in certain material respects, extraordinary. The infrastructure — libraries, laboratories, sports facilities, counseling centers, career services — represents an investment in the student experience that Nigerian institutions can only aspire to. A well-resourced American research university has resources that most Nigerian universities will not see in my lifetime.',
  'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1541339907198-e08756dedf3f?w=800&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format',
  'published',
  'Prof. Mahfouz A. Adedimeji'
);

